      "text": "To find the radius of the circle, we need to determine the distance from the center of the circle to one of the given points. The center of the circle is on the \\(x\\)-axis, so its coordinates are \\((h, 0)\\). The distance from the center \\((h, 0)\\) to the point \\((0, 4)\\) is the same as the distance from the center \\((h, 0)\\) to the point \\((1, 3)\\), because they both lie on the circle and the circle is symmetric about the \\(x\\)-axis. This distance is the radius of the circle.\n\nFirst, we calculate the distance from the center \\((h, 0)\\) to the point \\((0, 4)\\) using the distance formula:\n\\[\n\\text{Distance} = \\sqrt{(h - 0)^2 + (0 - 4)^2} = \\sqrt{h^2 + 16}\n\\]\nNext, we calculate the distance from the center \\((h, 0)\\) to the point \\((1, 3)\\) using the distance formula:\n\\[\n\\text{Distance} = \\sqrt{(h - 1)^2 + (0 - 3)^2} = \\sqrt{(h - 1)^2 + 9}\n\\]\nSince these two distances are equal (they are both the radius of the circle), we set them equal to each other:\n\\[\n\\sqrt{h^2 + 16} = \\sqrt{(h - 1)^2 + 9}\n\\]\nTo eliminate the square roots, we square both sides of the equation:\n\\[\nh^2 + 16 = (h - 1)^2 + 9\n\\]\nExpanding the right side, we get:\n\\[\nh^2 + 16 = h^2 - 2h + 1 + 9\n\\]\nSimplifying the right side, we get:\n\\[\nh^2 + 16 = h^2 - 2h + 10\n\\]\nSubtracting \\(h^2\\) from both sides, we get:\n\\[\n16 = -2h + 10\n\\]\nSubtracting 10 from both sides, we get:\n\\[\n6 = -2h\n\\]\nDividing both sides by -2, we get:\n\\[\nh = -3\n\\]\nSo, the center of the circle is \\((-3, 0)\\). Now, we find the radius by calculating the distance from the center \\((-3, 0)\\) to the point \\((0, 4)\\):\n\\[\n\\text{Radius} = \\sqrt{(-3 - 0)^2 + (0 - 4)^2} = \\sqrt{9 + 16} = \\sqrt{25} = 5\n\\]\nTherefore, the radius of the circle is \\(\\boxed{5}\\).",
