    {
      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "Given the points \\((0, 4)\\) and \\((1, 3)\\) lie on a circle whose center is on the \\(x\\)-axis, we start by finding the coordinates of the center of the circle. Let the center of the circle be \\((h, 0)\\).\n\nThe distance from the center \\((h, 0)\\) to both points \\((0, 4)\\) and \\((1, 3)\\) is the radius \\(r\\) of the circle. Using the distance formula, we can write the following equations for the distances:\n\n\\[\nr = \\sqrt{(h - 0)^2 + (0 - 4)^2} = \\sqrt{h^2 + 16}\n\\]\n\\[\nr = \\sqrt{(h - 1)^2 + (0 - 3)^2} = \\sqrt{(h - 1)^2 + 9}\n\\]\n\nSince both expressions represent the radius \\(r\\), we equate them:\n\n\\[\n\\sqrt{h^2 + 16} = \\sqrt{(h - 1)^2 + 9}\n\\]\n\nSquaring both sides to eliminate the square roots, we get:\n\n\\[\nh^2 + 16 = (h - 1)^2 + 9\n\\]\n\nExpanding the right side:\n\n\\[\nh^2 + 16 = h^2 - 2h + 1 + 9\n\\]\n\nSimplifying the right side:\n\n\\[\nh^2 + 16 = h^2 - 2h + 10\n\\]\n\nSubtracting \\(h^2\\) from both sides:\n\n\\[\n16 = -2h + 10\n\\]\n\nRearranging terms to solve for \\(h\\):\n\n\\[\n16 - 10 = -2h\n\\]\n\\[\n6 = -2h\n\\]\n\\[\nh = -3\n\\]\n\nSo, the center of the circle is \\((-3, 0)\\). Now, we find the radius \\(r\\) by calculating the distance from the center \\((-3, 0)\\) to one of the points on the circle, say \\((0, 4)\\):\n\n\\[\nr = \\sqrt{(-3 - 0)^2 + (0 - 4)^2} = \\sqrt{(-3)^2 + (-4)^2} = \\sqrt{9 + 16} = \\sqrt{25} = 5\n\\]\n\nThus, the radius of the circle is \\(\\boxed{5}\\).",
