    {
      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To determine the radius of the circle, we need to find the coordinates of the center and then use the distance from the center to one of the given points to calculate the radius. Here are the steps:\n\n1. **Identify the coordinates of the center of the circle:**\n   The center of the circle lies on the x-axis, so its coordinates are of the form \\((h, 0)\\).\n\n2. **Use the distance formula to set up equations:**\n   The distance from the center \\((h, 0)\\) to the point \\((0, 4)\\) is equal to the distance from the center \\((h, 0)\\) to the point \\((1, 3)\\). Let \\(r\\) be the radius of the circle. Then we have:\n   \\[\n   \\sqrt{(h - 0)^2 + (0 - 4)^2} = \\sqrt{(h - 1)^2 + (0 - 3)^2}\n   \\]\n   Simplifying both sides, we get:\n   \\[\n   \\sqrt{h^2 + 16} = \\sqrt{(h - 1)^2 + 9}\n   \\]\n   Squaring both sides to eliminate the square roots, we obtain:\n   \\[\n   h^2 + 16 = (h - 1)^2 + 9\n   \\]\n   Expanding the right side, we get:\n   \\[\n   h^2 + 16 = h^2 - 2h + 1 + 9\n   \\]\n   Simplifying further, we have:\n   \\[\n   h^2 + 16 = h^2 - 2h + 10\n   \\]\n   Subtracting \\(h^2\\) from both sides, we get:\n   \\[\n   16 = -2h + 10\n   \\]\n   Solving for \\(h\\), we get:\n   \\[\n   16 - 10 = -2h \\implies 6 = -2h \\implies h = -3\n   \\]\n   So, the center of the circle is \\((-3, 0)\\).\n\n3. **Calculate the radius:**\n   The radius \\(r\\) is the distance from the center \\((-3, 0)\\) to the point \\((0, 4)\\). Using the distance formula again, we have:\n   \\[\n   r = \\sqrt{(-3 - 0)^2 + (0 - 4)^2} = \\sqrt{9 + 16} = \\sqrt{25} = 5\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, the radius of the circle is \\(\\boxed{5}\\).",
